  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nSome showers and isolated thunderstorms have developed near the\nInterstate 71 corridor this evening. Expect some additional activity\nto develop generally near this corridor and points southeast for the\nevening into the first part of the night before dissipating. Already\nhad one report of a funnel cloud in Madison county earlier. Typically\nin this environment these funnels rarely touch down and if they do\nthey are very weak.  Some patchy fog will then be possible in river \nvalleys and some locations southeast of Interstate 71 overnight. \n\nAnomalous upper level ridging extends eastward into the Great Lakes \nand Ohio Valley this week while high pressure at the surface remains \nover the region. Hot conditions, with highs in the lower 90s are \nlikely through at least Thursday. Peak afternoon heat index values \nreach into the upper 90s to near 100 when factoring in dew points in \nthe upper 60s. The hottest day of the week will likely be Wednesday. \nBorderline Heat Advisory conditions are expected the middle of this \nweek with dew points being the main limiting factor. \n\nThe upper level ridge starts to break the end of this week when a \nlow pressure system is forecast to move through the Great Lakes. \nShower and storm chances increase with this low moving into the \nregion this weekend and temperatures cool slightly.",
